#!/bin/bash
# `timeout` is coreutils and the smaller agent images do without it. Refusing
# there would break every scaffolding to guard against a hang that may never
# happen, so the step degrades -- and says so, in the messages the developer is
# already reading.
#
# The curated PATH is the point: on CI `timeout` exists, so without it this case
# would pass by never reaching the branch it is meant to cover.
source "$(dirname "$0")/../lib.sh"
setup
trap teardown EXIT

mkdir -p "$TEST_TMP/nobin"
for binary in bash mktemp rm; do
  ln -s "$(command -v "$binary")" "$TEST_TMP/nobin/$binary"
done

seen="$TEST_TMP/seen"

cat > "$TEST_TMP/orchestrator.sh" <<EOS
#!/bin/bash
echo "scaffolded" > "$seen"
EOS

export TRIGGER_SCAFFOLD_SCRIPT="$TEST_TMP/orchestrator.sh"

FULL_PATH="$PATH"

# Replacing the search path is the assertion, not a slip: the branch under test
# is the one `command -v timeout` takes when there is no timeout to find.
# shellcheck disable=SC2123
PATH="$TEST_TMP/nobin"
run_code_repo_step scaffold_repository
PATH="$FULL_PATH"

assert_status 0 || exit 1
assert_contains "no 'timeout' on the agent host" || exit 1
assert_contains "(no timeout)" || exit 1

if [[ "$(cat "$seen" 2>/dev/null)" != "scaffolded" ]]; then
  echo "  FAIL: the scaffolding did not run when 'timeout' was unavailable"
  exit 1
fi
